// TEST: Let-bound kernel body deduces commutative (dense until pinned)
// WARN: BL4010
// THE flagship shape for stage-3's binding-form descent. Blade has no
// `let x = v in body` expression — the bind-then-use kernel is the brace block
// `{ let d = x - y  <newline>  d * d }` — and every parity walker used to
// bottom out on TExprBlock, so this kernel deduced NOTHING at all. The
// deduction now normalizes bindings away first: `d` is single-operator-small
// and used twice, so it inlines to (x - y) * (x - y). The two children are not
// each other's image under the swap, so the mirror rule stands down and the
// SIGN chain rule does the work instead — each factor is PNeg, and
// PNeg * PNeg = PInv.
//
// Both positions receive the same array, so the compiler PROPOSES the pin:
//   kernel deduces commutative in (x, y) and both positions receive the same
//   array: output storage is DENSE today. Pin `where comm(x, y)` on the kernel
//   to opt into compact symmetric (triangular) storage.
// (BL4010; asserted manually via `blade check` until the corpus `// SUGGEST:`
// block covers PinSuggestions as well as CertSuggestions.)
//
// Storage stays DENSE, because a deduction only ever proposes — the pinned
// twin is symmetry/029, and symmetry/030 is the machine-checked proof that the
// deduced parity is really PInv. Same relationship as 022 (dense) to 020
// (pinned).
let A = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
let result = method_for(A, A) <@> lambda(x, y) -> {
    let d = x - y
    d * d
} |> compute
// Full dense 3x3, printed flat, row-major: (A[i] - A[j])^2 for every (i, j).
// Symmetric about the diagonal, which is what the deduction just proved and
// what the triangular storage of 029 exploits.
// EXPECT: result = [0.0, 1.0, 4.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 4.0, 1.0, 0.0]
